/*****************************************************************************
    kant8N -- THE NULL PATH ACROSS THE RETURN SEAM. 2026-08-10, SEQ 27 rung A.

    THE CLAUSE THIS EXISTS FOR, and it is the only one of the three that fails
    quietly. The seam in runAction mints a fresh node and copies the value in.
    Clause three says a run that produced no result must hand back nothing at
    all, rather than a freshly minted empty node. The other two clauses have
    loud customers -- a caller that reads text off a bare number breaks at once,
    and a caller handed the local's own node reads it back blanked, which is the
    whole of the defect this campaign repaired. Clause three has neither. Two
    callers in the generated parse layer test their result for emptiness and
    then read it, so an empty node arriving in place of nothing stops the
    emptiness test firing and an empty answer is taken for a successful one.

    ⚠⚠ READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN RUN HERE, because the first draft of
    this file was green and certified NOTHING, and the negative control is the
    only reason anybody knows.

    THERE ARE TWO WAYS runAction ANSWERS WITH NOTHING, and they are not the same
    path. The reachable one is its early return, taken when a coded body fails
    to parse; that return happens ABOVE the seam and so preserves nothing-ness
    without the seam being involved at all. The other is a run that gets past
    that point and still yields no result, which is the one the seam's own guard
    covers. Row N3 below travels the FIRST path. It was written believing it
    travelled the second, and the control says otherwise: with the guard
    deliberately removed and the seam minting on the empty path, N3 DID NOT
    MOVE. A row that cannot go red is not evidence, so it is labelled here for
    what it actually measures rather than for what was hoped.

    AND THE SECOND PATH HAS NO ROW AT ALL, deliberately. A sweep of the entire
    fixture population measured it occurring zero times in 128 files, so there
    is no reachable case to build one from. Its guard stays because without it
    the mint would dereference nothing and take the process down. That is stated
    rather than papered over: an uncontrolled guard is a known gap, and a green
    row invented to cover it would be worse than the gap.

    WHAT THIS FILE THEREFORE CERTIFIES: that a call yielding nothing still
    yields nothing after crossing the seam, on the path the corpus actually
    reaches, with N0 as the anti-vacuity row proving the seam is running at all.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, exactly ONE stop.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(Kant8N);
define
    nOut;
    nTwo;
    nThree;
    nFour;
    nValued code={
        nGood = 99;
        return nGood;
        };
    nQuiet code={
        nInner = 5;
        };
    nPrinter code={
        print "N2 inside : the action ran, and its last act is this print":;
        };
    nEmpty code={
        };
    ;

print "== N0 ANTI-VACUITY: an action that DOES return a value ==":;
nThree = "stamped";
nThree = nValued();
print "N0 nThree =" nThree " (want 99; a stamped or cleared read means the seam is not running)":;

print "== N1: a body with no return at all -- NOT a null, it yields its last value ==":;
nOut = "stamped";
nOut = nQuiet();
print "N1 nOut =" nOut " (want 5; recorded because 'no return' is NOT the null path)":;

print "== N2: a body whose last act is a print -- also not a null ==":;
nTwo = "stamped";
nTwo = nPrinter();
print "N2 nTwo =" nTwo " (want 1; the print's own value, again not the null path)":;

print "== N3: THE REACHABLE NULL -- a body that fails to parse, so nothing comes back ==":;
nFour = "stamped";
nFour = nEmpty();
print "N3 nFour =" nFour " (want the cleared read nFour; stamped = never assigned; any mint tag = clause 3 broken)":;

print "kant8N SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
